I now have a complete picture. Here is my thorough report.

## Overview

This is the Spring PetClinic REST sample (`org.springframework.samples.petclinic`), Maven build (`/home/daniel/sandbox/pom.xml`). Everything related to owner identity lives in three areas: the `model` package (entities/audit), the `util` package (derivation helpers), and the v1 REST controller. There is a redesign task embedded in the test: the `customerCode` must change from its current `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` format to `<REGION>-<HASH8>`.

---

## 1. All files/classes that reference "customerCode"

| File | Lines | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` | 71-86 | Field definition + getter/setter + `assignCustomerCode(int)` builder |
| `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` | 113-114, 123 | Per-city sequence + calls `assignCustomerCode` |
| `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/CityCodes.java` | whole file | Counts owners per city -> the sequence number |
| `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` | 26, 31 | Derives `membershipNumber` and `checkDigit` from `customerCode` |
| `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/OwnerAuditListener.java` | 34 | Logs `customerCode` in audit entry |
| `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` | 2108-2113 | DTO schema (`customerCode`, `readOnly`) |
| `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/.../acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` | 11-14 | Test asserting new `<REGION>-<HASH8>` format |
| DB schemas: `db/{hsqldb,mysql,h2,postgres}/schema.sql` | `customer_code VARCHAR(20)`/`TEXT` | Column definition |

---

## 2. How customerCode is currently built

Definition in `Owner.java` (lines 71-86):
```java
@Column(name = "customer_code")
private String customerCode;

public String getCustomerCode() { return this.customerCode; }
public void setCustomerCode(String customerCode) { this.customerCode = customerCode; }

/** Assign the customer code '<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>' for the given per-city sequence number. */
public void assignCustomerCode(int sequence) {
    this.customerCode = getCity().substring(0, 3).toUpperCase() + "-"
        + getLastName().substring(0, 3).toUpperCase() + String.format("-%04d", sequence);
}
```
Note: the code actually produces `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (e.g. `SYD-SMI-0007`), while the OpenAPI doc at line 2110 describes it as `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (example `SMI-0007`) — a pre-existing mismatch.

The sequence number is the per-city owner count + 1, computed in the controller `addOwner` (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java` lines 109-123):
```java
Collection<Owner> owners = this.clinicService.findAllOwners();
...
if (CityCodes.count(owners, owner) >= 50) {           // line 113: per-city cap
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
}
...
owner.assignCustomerCode(CityCodes.count(owners, owner) + 1);   // line 123
```

The counter itself, `CityCodes.java` (lines 22-25):
```java
static int count(Collection<Owner> existing, Owner candidate) {
    String city = normalize(candidate.getCity());
    return (int) existing.stream().filter(o -> normalize(o.getCity()).equals(city)).count();
}
```
(`normalize` lower-cases, null->"", line 17-19.)

---

## 3. Membership number and check digit derivation

Both are derived in the MapStruct mapper `OwnerMapper.java` via `@Mapping` expressions:

Line 26 — membership number `<customerCode>-M<YY>`:
```java
@Mapping(target = "membershipNumber", expression = "java((owner.getCustomerCode() == null || owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) ? null : owner.getCustomerCode() + \"-M\" + String.format(\"%02d\", owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100))")
```

Line 31 — check digit (Luhn over customerCode digits):
```java
@Mapping(target = "checkDigit", expression = "java(owner.getCustomerCode() == null ? null : org.springframework.samples.petclinic.util.Luhn.checkDigit(owner.getCustomerCode()))")
```

The Luhn implementation, `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/Luhn.java` (final class, single static method `checkDigit(String)`, lines 12-28):
```java
public static int checkDigit(String s) {
    int sum = 0;
    boolean dbl = true;
    for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        char c = s.charAt(i);
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') { continue; }
        int d = c - '0';
        if (dbl && (d *= 2) > 9) { d -= 9; }
        sum += d;
        dbl = !dbl;
    }
    return (10 - (sum % 10)) % 10;
}
```
Also note `membershipLevel` (line 27): starts at 1, +1 if email present, +1 if `namesakeCount==0`; the controller `getOwner` bumps it to 4 for household tenure (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java` line 98-100).

---

## 4. Create audit record code for owners

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/OwnerAuditListener.java` — a JPA entity listener wired to `Owner` via `@EntityListeners(OwnerAuditListener.class)` (`Owner.java` line 39). Full logic (lines 28-37):
```java
public class OwnerAuditListener {
    private static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger("AUDIT");

    @PostPersist
    void onCreate(Owner owner) {
        AUDIT.info("owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}",
            owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());
    }
}
```
It fires on `@PostPersist` and logs to a dedicated `AUDIT` logger. Tests capture this via `AuditLogCapture` (see section 9).

---

## 5. Locality derivation code

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/Localities.java` (abstract class, 2 public static `regionOf` overloads + 1 private helper):
- Line 30-31: `CITY_REGION` map `Sydney->NSW, Melbourne->VIC, Brisbane->QLD`.
- Line 33-35: `POSTCODE_REGION` map keyed by leading 2 digits `20->NSW, 30->VIC, 40->QLD`.
- Line 41-43: `regionOf(String city)` -> table lookup, default `"UNKNOWN"`.
- Line 53-56: `regionOf(String city, String postcode)` -> prefers postcode, falls back to city.
- Line 58-63: `regionOfPostcode` -> requires 4-digit postcode, `parseInt/100` lookup.

Invoked from the mapper `OwnerMapper.java` line 28:
```java
@Mapping(target = "locality", expression = "java(org.springframework.samples.petclinic.util.Localities.regionOf(owner.getCity(), owner.getPostcode()))")
```

---

## 6. Region code / postcode handling

- Entity field: `Owner.java` lines 57-66 — `@Column(name="postcode") private String postcode;` + getter/setter. DB column `postcode VARCHAR(4)` (schema.sql files).
- Region derivation: `Localities.java` (section 5).
- Validation: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/PostcodeValidation.java`. A class-level bean-validation constraint `@PostcodeValidation` (lines 27-34) plus validator `PostcodeRegionValidator` (lines 37-57):
  - Line 39-42: `REGION_RANGE` map `NSW=[2000,2099], VIC=[3000,3099], QLD=[4000,4099]`.
  - Line 45-56: `isValid` — a 4-digit postcode must fall in the range of the city's region (`Localities.regionOf(city)`); absent/non-4-digit postcode or unknown region passes.

---

## 7. SHA-256 / hashing utilities already present

There is **no** SHA-256 or hashing utility in `src/main` — the `util` package contains only: `Addresses`, `BusinessDays`, `CallMonitoringAspect`, `E164`, `EntityUtils`, `IdentityKeys`, `Localities`, `Luhn`. No `MessageDigest`/`DigestUtils`/`Hashing` usage in main code.

The only SHA-256 code lives in the **test** base class `AcceptanceBase.java` (lines 281-299), which the new `<REGION>-<HASH8>` redesign will need to mirror:
```java
protected static String sha256hex(String s) {
    byte[] digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256").digest(s.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(digest.length * 2);
    for (byte b : digest) { sb.append(String.format("%02x", b)); }
    return sb.toString();
}
/** First n UPPER-case hex characters of SHA-256(s). */
protected static String shaHex(String s, int n) {
    return sha256hex(s).substring(0, n).toUpperCase();
}
```
So the expected new format is likely `<REGION>-<first 8 upper-hex of SHA-256(...)>` (test regex `[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}`).

